# SHA-256 of every upstream file this SDK ships unmodified from the official
# cJSON 1.7.19 release tarball, together with every fixture byte its tests read
# at run time.
#
#   https://github.com/DaveGamble/cJSON/archive/refs/tags/v1.7.19.tar.gz
#   tag v1.7.19, tag commit c859b25da02955fef659d658b8f324b5cde87be3
#   release published 2025-09-09T13:56:10Z, 356247 bytes
#   sha256 7fa616e3046edfa7a28a32d5f9eacfd23f92900fe1f8ccd988c1662f30454562
#
# The release digest is recorded here and in UPSTREAM.md so the two files cannot
# disagree about which release this is. It is deliberately not a line for
# `sha256sum -c`: the tarball is not shipped, the extracted tree under `src/` is,
# and every byte of that tree is listed below.
#
# `src/` is upstream's, whole and unmodified: the library source and headers,
# the companion cJSON_Utils pair, the complete test suite with the Unity test
# framework it vendors and the JSON Patch corpus it vendors, the fuzzing
# harnesses, the library configuration document, and upstream's own build files,
# README, CHANGELOG, CONTRIBUTORS, SECURITY policy and AppVeyor configuration.
# Nothing was excluded and nothing was edited, so the file-set check in
# tools/check-provenance.sh is a plain comparison of two `find` outputs.
#
# `testdata/` is this fork's own fixture corpus, recorded here for the same
# reason: a fixture whose bytes changed under a test would be a test that
# quietly stopped testing anything. The provenance of each file -- RFC 8259 for
# the five documents under `testdata/rfc8259/`, the fork's own bytes for the rest,
# and simdjson's upstream example corpus for `testdata/large/twitter.json` -- is
# in UPSTREAM.md, section "Fixture corpus".
#
